PART 7 — STRATEGY RESEARCH AND VALIDATION

Three candidate strategy families must be compared fairly.

No strategy is pre-approved as the final winner.

STRATEGY A — Trend-Aligned Pullback

Evaluate:

- Higher-timeframe direction.
- Pullback definition.
- Valid setup conditions.
- Entry confirmation.
- Invalidation.
- Stop-loss.
- Take-profit.
- Market regime suitability.

STRATEGY B — Breakout & Retest

Evaluate:

- Valid breakout definition.
- Closing confirmation.
- Retest definition.
- Failed breakout conditions.
- Entry trigger.
- Invalidation.
- Stop-loss.
- Take-profit.

STRATEGY C — Liquidity Sweep & Reversal

Evaluate:

- Liquidity level definition.
- Sweep definition.
- Reclaim conditions.
- Structure confirmation.
- Displacement confirmation.
- Entry trigger.
- Invalidation.
- Stop-loss.
- Take-profit.

7.1 Fair testing protocol

All three strategies must be tested under comparable conditions.

Testing must include:

- Identical data quality requirements.
- Identical date ranges where possible.
- Comparable spread assumptions.
- Commission.
- Swap where applicable.
- Slippage assumptions.
- Real-tick backtesting where available.
- Out-of-sample testing.
- Walk-forward validation.
- Different market regimes.
- Drawdown analysis.
- Trade sample-size analysis.
- Parameter sensitivity.
- Robustness checks.
- Overfitting detection.
- Risk-adjusted performance.

Do not choose the strategy with the highest historical net profit alone.

Evaluate stability, drawdown, execution sensitivity, sample size, and out-of-sample performance.

A strategy must not be declared profitable merely because one backtest looks good.

The intended later validation sequence includes:

1. Historical backtesting.
2. Out-of-sample testing.
3. Walk-forward testing.
4. Robustness testing.
5. Demo forward evaluation for approximately 8–12 weeks.

The final strategy selection requires evidence and Founder approval.

